In Fig.~\ref{Fig:Pat_UEA}(a), the width of each rectangle represents the time ratio of the particular active pattern.
The number of active patterns is $4$, which satisfies Proposition~\ref{Pro:pattern_number}. 
Fig.~\ref{Fig:Pat_UEA}(b) shows that the association is consistent with our intuitive result and the brute-force solution.
The orthogonal scheme in Fig.~\ref{Fig:CDF} considers the patterns where only the BS and one D2D relay active, i.e. $\mathcal V = \{ \bm{e}_1 + \bm{e}_{u+1} \}_{ u \in \mathcal{U}}$.
It is worth noting that some D2D effective rates given by the orthogonal scheme are higher than the SDPS solution. This phenomenon indicates that the SDPS algorithm can select DUEs with high data rate as relays to help other DUEs in poor channel state and balance the resource allocation. 
In particular,  the SDPS algorithm achieves higher geometry mean (GE) rate than the other two schemes.



\section{Conclusion} \label{Conclusion}
We have studied the optimal time reuse in a HetNet with D2D relays. By decoupling all DUEs into ``servers'' and ``users'', a large scale integer programming to maximize the PF of all DUE effective rates is formulated. 
We show that the optimal solution can be derived from a small number of patterns instead of the exponentially increasing number of patterns.   
One semi-distributed algorithm is put forward to find the particular pattern set and solve the problem approximately. 
Numerical results corroborate our designs.



